From 2fa2abced5fa893fa6a09d93cffdddc6b6d52adf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: f <f@f>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 22:17:38 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] surfaces: take I420 on the encoder, the format FFmpeg uploads
 in

FFmpeg, and with it RustDesk and every other client built on libavutil,
uploads a frame from memory by having the driver make surfaces with their
pixel format set to I420 and writing the three planes straight into them
(av_hwframe_transfer_data(), which maps the surface with vaDeriveImage()).
The driver made NV12 surfaces only and refused those: "surface pixel
format does not match RT format". The way it did take, an I420 image put
onto an NV12 surface with vaPutImage(), is not what these clients use.

So with this driver RustDesk could not have opened its encoder. Run by
ffmpeg 8.0.1 with the settings RustDesk gives h264_vaapi: refused. With
the frames turned into NV12 first, and nothing else changed, the same
settings encode testsrc2 at 145-159 fps at 720p, 107-122 at 1080p and 84
at 2340x1080.

An I420 surface is the allocation an NV12 surface always had, read as
three planes: U after the luma plane, V after U, each with half the pitch
and half the rows. vaDeriveImage() gives an image in three planes, so the
client writes in place and converts nothing. vaGetImage() and vaPutImage()
take I420 and NV12 images on either side of an I420 surface. The encoder
interleaves the chroma into the NV12 input it hands the hardware, in
copy_surface(), in the pass in which it copied the frame anyway. I420 is
offered for encoder configs only: decoders and the video processor still
take NV12 and P010, and an I420 surface cannot be exported.

tests/encoder/i420-surface.cpp holds the encoder path against the way that
has always worked: the same 96 frames put onto NV12 with vaPutImage() and
written straight into I420 must be one bitstream. And the image paths
must bring every sample back where it was put, U and V not swapped.
